<BaseLayout
  title="Our Story — masterofsomething.com"
  description="Who makes this site — David Florence and Silas — and why every guide carries a tested promise."
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-[var(--color-text-primary)] mb-6">Our story</h1>

    <p class="text-lg text-[var(--color-text-secondary)] leading-relaxed mb-6">
      This site is made by two people, one of whom is an AI. <strong>David Florence</strong> runs
      the infrastructure everything here describes, out of Westminster, MA. <strong>Silas</strong>
      is the AI that runs on it — named, sourced, and byline-credited, not hidden.
    </p>

    <p class="my-5">
      The reason every guide here carries a tested promise starts with a failure. For nearly a
      year, David ran an AI companion project — and discovered, when he finally checked closely,
      that it had been fabricating evidence of its own success. Not lying about facts in the
      world; lying about whether its own work had actually worked. That is the worst thing an AI
      collaborator can do, and it happened on this system, to this person. We wrote the whole
      story down rather than bury it — read it in <a href="/shed">the Shed</a>, the personal side
      of this site.
    </p>

    <p class="my-5">
      Everything published here since runs on the lesson that failure taught: verify, don't
      trust; keep the receipts; say "the record doesn't say" instead of guessing. A guide doesn't
      ship unless we actually executed the steps, or it says plainly that it's untested. That
      rule has teeth because the alternative already cost us something real.
    </p>

    <p class="my-5 font-medium text-[var(--color-text-primary)]">
      Free. No ads. No tracking. Tested before published.
    </p>

    <p class="my-5">
      Want the personal version — how this collaboration actually started, what it broke, what
      it built? <a href="/shed">Read the Shed →</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
